\title{Complementation Orbits in Finite Framed Jigsaw Assembly Spaces}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 26, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study global tab--blank complementation for finite framed jigsaw puzzles. The geometric input is deliberately minimal: a puzzle has a finite set of complete assemblies, a complemented puzzle is obtained by reversing all non-flat edge polarities, and this operation induces a bijection between the two complete assembly spaces. From this input we derive the full orbit structure on their tagged disjoint union. The induced complement map is an involution, has no fixed points, and partitions the combined space into two-element orbits. Hence the original and complemented assembly spaces have equal cardinality, while their tagged union has even cardinality. Contrary to a natural preliminary conjecture, no non-self-duality hypothesis is needed: even when a puzzle is isomorphic to its complement, the side tag changes under complementation and rules out fixed points. A singleton self-dual system demonstrates the sharp distinction between a tagged coproduct and an untagged identification. We also describe enumeration and validation algorithms and explain applications to parsimonious reductions and counting problems.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
A framed edge-matching jigsaw consists of pieces whose sides carry local compatibility data. In the motivating model, every side is flat, a colored tab, or a colored blank. A tab fits a blank when their colors agree; equal polarities do not fit. Pieces may be constrained not to rotate, and a fixed rectangular frame prescribes the exterior boundary. Such systems are geometric constraint networks: a complete assembly is a global witness assembled from locally compatible contacts.

Global complementation reverses every non-flat polarity. Tabs become blanks, blanks become tabs, and colors and positions are retained. Complementing both edges at an interior contact preserves the relation ``opposite polarity with equal color,'' so complete assemblies should correspond to complete assemblies of the complemented puzzle. The essential geometric task in any concrete model is to establish this correspondence as a bijection.

This paper isolates the consequences once that bijection is available. Complementation on the tagged union is an involution, has no fixed points, and has only two-element orbits. The two finite assembly spaces consequently have equal size and their tagged union has even size. These conclusions remain true for self-dual puzzles. The distinction between tagged and untagged spaces is decisive: if the two copies are identified through a chosen self-duality, fixed points may appear after identification, whereas before quotienting the tags force a free action.

\section{Definitions and geometric model}

\begin{definition}[Finite framed puzzle system]
A finite framed puzzle system consists of a collection $\mathcal P$ of puzzles; a finite set $A(P)$ of complete framed assemblies for each $P\in\mathcal P$; a complement operation $P\mapsto P^c$ satisfying $(P^c)^c=P$; and, for each $P$, an assembly-complement bijection
\[
c_P:A(P)\longrightarrow A(P^c).
\]
\end{definition}

The map $c_P$ is expected to reverse tabs and blanks throughout a completed placement while preserving colors, locations, multiplicities, and flat exterior edges. The inverse of $c_P$ transports assemblies in the other direction.

\begin{definition}[Tagged combined assembly space]
For a fixed puzzle $P$, define
\[
C(P)=\bigl(\{L\}\times A(P)\bigr)\sqcup
\bigl(\{R\}\times A(P^c)\bigr).
\]
The tags record which puzzle an assembly belongs to. Elements with different tags remain distinct even when their underlying structures are isomorphic.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Tagged complement transformation]
Define $T_P:C(P)\to C(P)$ by
\[
T_P(L,a)=(R,c_P(a)),\qquad
T_P(R,b)=(L,c_P^{-1}(b)).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Involution and freeness]
A map $T:X\to X$ is an involution if $T(T(x))=x$ for every $x$. A fixed point satisfies $T(x)=x$. An involution acts freely if it has no fixed points. Its orbit through $x$ is $\operatorname{Orb}_T(x)=\{x,T(x)\}$.
\end{definition}

For the concrete square-piece model, write a non-rotatable piece as an ordered tuple $(e_N,e_E,e_S,e_W)$. Each edge is flat or a pair $(\kappa,\sigma)$, where $\kappa$ is a color and $\sigma\in\{+1,-1\}$ is polarity. Set
\[
(\kappa,\sigma)^c=(\kappa,-\sigma)
\]
and leave flat edges unchanged. Two non-flat edges match when their colors agree and their polarities sum to zero. Complementing both members of a compatible pair preserves compatibility. A consistently complemented frame likewise preserves boundary contacts.

To obtain the assumed bijection in a fully specified puzzle model, one must also check that piece identities or multiplicities are transported correctly, that edge order remains fixed when rotation is forbidden, and that no quotient convention creates or destroys placements. Once these checks are made, applying edgewise complementation twice proves injectivity and surjectivity simultaneously.

\section{Orbit structure and counting}

\begin{theorem}[Involutivity]
For every puzzle $P$, the tagged complement transformation $T_P$ is an involution on $C(P)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $(L,a)$, two applications give
\[
T_P^2(L,a)=(L,c_P^{-1}(c_P(a)))=(L,a).
\]
For $(R,b)$, they give
\[
T_P^2(R,b)=(R,c_P(c_P^{-1}(b)))=(R,b).
\]
These are the two cases in the tagged union.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Fixed-point freeness]
For every puzzle $P$ and every $x\in C(P)$, one has $T_P(x)\ne x$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A left-tagged element maps to a right-tagged element, and a right-tagged element maps to a left-tagged element. Distinct tags cannot be equal.
\end{proof}

This proof uses no geometric asymmetry between $P$ and $P^c$. Freeness comes solely from the coproduct tag.

\begin{theorem}[Two-element orbit theorem]
For every $x\in C(P)$, the orbit $\{x,T_P(x)\}$ has cardinality two.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Involutivity implies that the orbit has at most the two displayed elements. Fixed-point freeness says they are distinct.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Equality of assembly counts]
For every finite framed puzzle $P$,
\[
|A(P)|=|A(P^c)|.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The assembly-complement map $c_P$ is a bijection between the two finite sets.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Parity theorem]
For every finite framed puzzle $P$, the cardinality of $C(P)$ is even. More precisely,
\[
|C(P)|=2|A(P)|=2|A(P^c)|.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Cardinality is additive on tagged disjoint unions, and the preceding theorem equates the summands:
\[
|C(P)|=|A(P)|+|A(P^c)|=2|A(P)|.
\]
Equivalently, the two-element orbit theorem partitions $C(P)$ into pairs.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Orbit count]
The number of complementation orbits in $C(P)$ is exactly $|A(P)|$, and equally $|A(P^c)|$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Every orbit contains exactly one left-tagged member. Selecting it identifies the orbit set with $A(P)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Solvability equivalence]
The original puzzle has a complete assembly if and only if its complement does.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
A bijection preserves emptiness and nonemptiness.
\end{proof}

Solvability equivalence is strictly weaker than equality of counts: it forgets both the number and identity of witnesses.

\section{Self-duality and tagged versus untagged spaces}

\begin{definition}[Self-dual puzzle]
A puzzle $P$ is self-dual if it is isomorphic, under the chosen notion of puzzle equivalence, to $P^c$. Such an isomorphism may arise through relabeling pieces, geometric symmetry, or an explicit identification of the descriptions.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Self-duality does not obstruct tagged freeness]
The map $T_P$ acts freely on $C(P)$ even when $P$ is self-dual.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Self-duality may identify underlying puzzle structures, but it does not identify $L$ and $R$ in the tagged disjoint union. Since $T_P$ exchanges these tags, it fixes no tagged point.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[Self-dual singleton]
Let there be one puzzle $P$ with $P^c=P$, let $A(P)=\{\ast\}$, and let $c_P$ be the identity. Then
\[
C(P)=\{(L,\ast),(R,\ast)\},
\]
and complementation swaps its two elements. Thus the action is free and $|C(P)|=2$, despite maximal self-duality.
\end{example}

On the untagged singleton, the identity fixes $\ast$. The tag is therefore structural rather than cosmetic.

\begin{proposition}[Fixed points after untagging]
Suppose $P$ is self-dual and choose a bijection $s:A(P^c)\to A(P)$ to identify the right space with the left. The induced untagged map $U=s\circ c_P$ on $A(P)$ need not be fixed-point-free.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
In the singleton example both maps are identities, so $U$ fixes $\ast$. In general, the tagged theory does not exclude solutions of $s(c_P(a))=a$.
\end{proof}

Thus freeness is unconditional on the coproduct but must be reconsidered after any quotient or identification.

\section{Algorithms and finite examples}

\subsection{Complement transport}
Represent an assembly by $m$ placed pieces, each with four ordered edges. Traverse the pieces, preserve every placement, leave flat edges unchanged, and negate the polarity of each colored edge. This requires $O(m)$ time and $O(m)$ output space under constant-size edge records. A second traversal restores the input. Consequently, once all assemblies of $P$ are known, all assemblies of $P^c$ can be generated without a new combinatorial search.

\subsection{Orbit enumeration}
Given $A(P)$ and the map $c_P$, iterate only over left assemblies. For each $a\in A(P)$, output
\[
\{(L,a),(R,c_P(a))\}.
\]
This emits every orbit once because each orbit contains exactly one left member. If evaluating $c_P$ costs $q$, the running time is $O(|A(P)|q)$ plus output cost. No visited-set is required.

\subsection{Consistency audit}
For explicit finite lists and purported forward and inverse maps, verify that images lie in the opposite list and that both compositions are identities. These checks establish bijectivity. Equal side counts and even combined count are then redundant mathematical consequences but valuable diagnostics for malformed frames, asymmetric polarity handling, missing assemblies, or duplicates. With canonical hashable encodings, the audit is expected linear time in the total listed data, apart from complement-evaluation cost.

If either side contains $n$ assemblies, the exact values are
\[
|A(P^c)|=n,\qquad |C(P)|=2n,\qquad
|C(P)/\langle T_P\rangle|=n.
\]
The initial cases are shown below.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{rrrr}
\toprule
$n$ & $|A(P^c)|$ & $|C(P)|$ & orbit count\\
\midrule
0&0&0&0\\
1&1&2&1\\
2&2&4&2\\
3&3&6&3\\
4&4&8&4\\
5&5&10&5\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

These values illustrate the theorem; they are not evidence from which it is extrapolated.

\section{Applications}

\subsection{Counting complexity}
Decision reductions preserve whether a witness exists, whereas counting reductions seek to preserve the number of witnesses. A bijection is therefore stronger than equisatisfiability and is the natural basis of a parsimonious reduction. If framed puzzle assemblies correspond bijectively to satisfying assignments, complementation transports the complete witness set with no change in cardinality. Any discrepancy in a geometric gadget construction must occur before the orbit theorem applies, through unintended assemblies, lost boundary constraints, interchangeable copies, or gadget automorphisms.

\subsection{Symmetry-aware storage}
One may store a single representative per orbit, always choosing the left-tagged member. This reduces storage for the combined space by exactly a factor of two, aside from encoding overhead. Unlike an arbitrary lexicographic rule, the representative choice is intrinsic to the tagged construction.

\subsection{Metamorphic testing}
Complementary puzzles form exact test pairs. An enumerator should report equal counts for $P$ and $P^c$, and a generator of all tagged assemblies should return an even count. No independently tabulated answer is needed. Violations reveal asymmetric tab--blank treatment, frame conversion errors, orientation mistakes, or duplicate mishandling.

\subsection{Cancellation of antisymmetric statistics}
Let $f:C(P)\to G$ take values in an abelian group and satisfy $f(T_P(x))=-f(x)$. Then
\[
\sum_{x\in C(P)} f(x)=0.
\]
Indeed, every two-element orbit contributes $f(x)+f(T_P(x))=0$. The parity theorem is the simplest instance of the general principle that a free involution organizes global cancellation.

\section{Scope and limitations}
The abstract result is not itself a concrete realization of every jigsaw model. Finiteness is required for ordinary cardinal parity. Edgewise reversal must define a genuine bijection on complete assemblies: complementing pieces but not the frame may destroy boundary contacts; differing rotation rules may change placements; directed color semantics may require a color transformation; and quotients by indistinguishable pieces must be respected.

The tagged hypothesis is equally important. Passing to an untagged quotient can collapse paired points and alter orbit sizes, so stabilizers and fixed points must then be analyzed anew. Finally, an even total count does not imply the existence of a natural complement bijection. The bijection is the substantive structural input; parity is its consequence.

\section{Future directions}
The immediate goal is a concrete model of non-rotatable square pieces with flat, colored-tab, and colored-blank edges, multiplicities, rectangular placements, and a rigid frame. Componentwise complementation should then be proved bijective on complete placements.

A second direction concerns quotients by interchangeable pieces and geometric symmetries. Complementation descends when it respects equivalence classes, but freeness after identification is subtler. A classification of quotients preserving two-element orbits would mark the boundary between harmless representation choices and symmetry-induced fixed points.

A third direction is parsimonious geometric complexity. Planar wire, crossover, fan-out, and clause gadgets should make the number of rectangular assemblies equal the number of satisfying assignments, perhaps up to a formula-independent symmetry factor. Complement transport would then supply paired counting instances automatically.

A fourth direction extends local cancellation to topology. On an orientable surface, signed edge potentials may define a cohomology class. Interior pairing imposes a discrete divergence law, while noncontractible cycles can carry residual flux. On a torus, two cycle directions suggest two independent obstructions beyond rectangular tab--blank conservation.

Finally, one may study solution complexes rather than vertex sets alone. If single-variable flips correspond to bounded local rearrangements, a construction could preserve adjacency, connected components, or homotopy type. The present bijection controls vertices; the next challenge is to transport reconfiguration structure.

\section{Conclusion}
Once global tab--blank complementation induces a bijection between finite assembly spaces, its tagged orbit theory is rigid. The induced map is an involution without fixed points; every orbit has two elements; the two sides have equal size; and their combined count is even. Non-self-duality is unnecessary. Self-dual puzzles can acquire fixed points only after the tags are identified, as the singleton example demonstrates.

This separation of geometry from orbit theory makes the result reusable. A concrete model bears one burden: construct the assembly-complement bijection. Witness transport, equal counting, pair decomposition, and parity then follow together.

\end{document}

